Sending keys: ['cat > /app/NOTE.md << \'ENDOFFILE\'\n# Compiler Principles — Chapter 5: Semantic Analysis\n## Study Notes\n\n---\n\n## 1. Semantic Analysis Overview\n\nThe **semantic analysis** phase of a compiler:\n- Connects variable definitions to their uses.\n- Checks that each expression has the correct type.\n- Translates the abstract syntax into a simpler representation suitable for generating machine code.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Symbol Tables — Definitions & Differences\n\nSymbol tables (also called *environments*) map identifiers to their types and locations during semantic analysis.\n- **Declarations** of identifiers: IDs are *bound* to "meanings" in the symbol tables.\n- **Uses** of identifiers: look up IDs in the symbol tables.\n\nAn environment is a set of bindings denoted by the ↦ arrow, e.g., `σ0 = {g ↦ string, a ↦ int}`.\n\n### 2.1 Functional Symbol Table\n\n**Definition:** In the functional style, you *keep the old table pristine* while creating a new table with the additional binding. You never modify an existing symbol table.\n\n**Insertion Operation:**\n- Computing `σ\' = σ + {a ↦ τ}` does **not** change `σ`.\n- A completely new table is created by computing the "sum" of the existing table and the new binding.\n- Since the original table must still be available for lookups, nothing can be destroyed or modified.\n\n**Advantages:**\n- Easy to restore previous environments because they were never altered.\n- Pure functional — no side effects.\n\n**Disadvantages:**\n- Inefficient with hash tables: adding one binding to a hash table means copying the entire array.\n- For balanced binary search trees, inserting at depth `d` requires creating `d` new nodes — but only those nodes need to be copied (not the whole tree). Lookup is `O(log n)`.\n\n### 2.2 Imperative Symbol Table\n\n**Definition:** In the imperative style, you *modify the existing table in place* to build new bindings. The old table is mutated until it becomes the new one.\n\n**Insertion Operation:**\n- Computing `σ\' = σ + {a ↦ τ}` directly *modifies* `σ` — the new binding `a ↦ τ` is inserted as a new entry.\n- When shadows an existing binding (e.g., `a ↦ τ1` exists, and we insert `a ↦ τ2`), the new binding is placed at the **head** of the collision chain, effectively shadowing the old one: `hash(a) → <a, τ2> → <a, τ1>`.\n- To "restore" the old table, an **undo stack** is used: the old state is saved, and when exiting a scope, modifications are reversed by popping entries.\n\n**Advantages:**\n- Very efficient with hash tables: insertion and lookup are `O(1)` amortized.\n- No need to copy the entire data structure.\n\n**Disadvantages:**\n- When the new table exists, you cannot simultaneously look up things in the old one (it has been mutated).\n- Must maintain a separate undo mechanism.\n\n### 2.3 Key Differences in Insertion Operations\n\n| Aspect | Functional Style | Imperative Style |\n|---|---|---|\n| Modifies old table? | **No** — old table stays pristine | **Yes** — old table is mutated |\n| How insertion works | Creates new table by "summing" old + new binding | Inserts binding directly into existing hash table |\n| Restoring old state | Already available — never changed | Undo via an **undo stack** (or marksym) |\n| Best underlying structure | Balanced binary search tree (copy path from root) | Hash table with external chaining |\n| Insertion cost | `O(depth)` nodes copied | `O(1)` amortized |\n| Lookup cost | `O(log n)` for balanced BST | `O(1)` amortized for hash table |\n\n### 2.4 Scope Management\n\nEach local variable has a scope in which it is visible. As the semantic analysis reaches the end of each scope, the identifier bindings local to that scope are discarded.\n\n**Example:**\n```\n1 function f(a:int, b:int, c:int) =\n2   (print_int(a+c);\n3    let var j := a+b\n4             var a := "hello"\n5     in print(a); print_int(j)\n6    end;\n7    print_int(b)\n7   )\n```\n- Line 1: `σ1 = σ0 + {a ↦ int, b ↦ int, c ↦ int}`\n- Line 3: `σ2 = σ1 + {j ↦ int}`\n- Line 4: `σ3 = σ2 + {a ↦ string}` (a is now shadowed)\n- Line 6: discard `σ3`, go back to `σ1`\n- Line 7: look up `b` in `σ1`\n- Line 8: discard `σ1`, go back to `σ0`\n\nBindings in the right-hand table override those in the left: `X + Y ≠ Y + X`.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Code from the Presentation with Explanations\n\n### 3.1 Multiple Symbol Tables — Java Example\n\n```java\npackage M;\nclass E {\n    static int a = 5;\n}\nclass N {\n    static int b = 10;\n    static int a = E.a + b;\n}\nclass D {\n    static int d = E.a + N.a;\n}\n```\n\n**Explanation:** In Java, each class/module has its own symbol table. Forward references are allowed, so all classes E, N, and D are compiled together in a combined environment.\n\n**Symbol tables:**\n- `σ1 = {a ↦ int}` — for class E\n- `σ2 = {E ↦ σ1}` — E\'s own table\n- `σ3 = {b ↦ int, a ↦ int}` — for class N\n- `σ4 = {N ↦ σ3}` — N\'s own table\n- `σ5 = {d ↦ int}` — for class D\n- `σ6 = {D ↦ σ5}` — D\'s own table\n- `σ7 = σ2 + σ4 + σ6` — combined package environment\n\nThe result of the analysis is `{M ↦ σ7}`.\n\n### 3.2 Multiple Symbol Tables — ML Example\n\n```sml\nstructure M = struct\n  structure E = struct\n    val a = 5;\n  end\n  structure N = struct\n    val b = 10\n    val a = E.a + b\n  end\n  structure D = struct\n    val d = E.a + N.a\n  end\nend\n```\n\n**Explanation:** Similar to Java, each structure has its own symbol table. N is compiled using `σ0 + σ2`, and D is compiled using `σ0 + σ2 + σ4`. The result is `{M ↦ σ7}`.\n\n---\n\n### 3.3 Imperative Hash Table — Hash Function\n\n```c\nstruct bucket {\n    string key;\n    void *binding;\n    struct bucket *next;\n};\n#define SIZE 109\nstruct bucket *table[SIZE];\n\nunsigned int hash(char *s0)\n{\n    unsigned int h = 0;\n    char *s;\n    for (s = s0; *s; s++)\n        h = h * 65599 + *s;\n    return h;\n}\n```\n\n**Explanation:** A hash table with `SIZE = 109` buckets is used. The hash function uses a polynomial rolling hash: `h = (α^(n-1)*c1 + α^(n-2)*c2 + ... + α*cn-1 + cn)` where α = 65599 and `ci` is the i-th character. Collisions are resolved using external chaining (linked lists of `bucket` structs).\n\n### 3.4 Imperative Hash Table — Bucket Constructor\n\n```c\nstruct bucket *Bucket(string key, void *binding, struct bucket *next) {\n    struct bucket *b = checked_malloc(sizeof(*b));\n    b->key = key;\n    b->binding = binding;\n    b->next = next;\n    return b;\n}\n```\n\n**Explanation:** Allocates a new bucket node and initializes it with the key, binding, and next pointer. Used to prepend new entries to a collision chain.\n\n### 3.5 Imperative Hash Table — Insert\n\n```c\nvoid insert(string key, void *binding) {\n    int index = hash(key) % SIZE;\n    table[index] = Bucket(key, binding, table[index]);\n}\n```\n\n**Explanation:** Computes the hash index, then creates a new bucket at the **head** of the collision chain. This way, the newest binding for a given key shadows any older ones. For example, if `a ↦ τ1` exists and we insert `a ↦ τ2`, the chain becomes: `hash(a) → <a, τ2> → <a, τ1>`.\n\n### 3.6 Imperative Hash Table — Lookup\n\n```c\nvoid *lookup(string key) {\n    int index = hash(key) % SIZE;\n    struct bucket *b;\n    for (b = table[index]; b; b = b->next)\n        if (0 == strcmp(b->key, key))\n            return b->binding;\n    return NULL;\n}\n```\n\n**Explanation:** Computes the hash index, then traverses the collision chain to find the matching key. Returns the binding of the **first** (most recently inserted) match found, which is the correct one due to the shadowing behavior of head-of-chain insertion.\n\n### 3.7 Imperative Hash Table — Pop\n\n```c\nvoid pop(string key) {\n    int index = hash(key) % SIZE;\n    table[index] = table[index]->next;\n}\n```\n\n**Explanation:** Removes the **head** of the collision chain, which is the most recently inserted binding. This restores the environment to its previous state for that key. Works because insert always prepends to the chain (stack-like behavior).\n\n### 3.8 Tiger Compiler — Symbol Interning\n\n```c\nstatic S_symbol mksymbol(string name, S_symbol next) {\n    S_symbol s = checked_malloc(sizeof(*s));\n    s->name = name;\n    s->next = next;\n    return s;\n}\n\nS_symbol S_symbol(string name) {\n    int index = hash(name) % SIZE;\n    S_symbol syms = hashtable[index], sym;\n    for (sym = syms; sym; sym = sym->next)\n        if (0 == strcmp(sym->name, name)) return sym;\n    sym = mksymbol(name, syms);\n    hashtable[index] = sym;\n    return sym;\n}\n\nstring S_name(S_symbol sym) {\n    return sym->name;\n}\n```\n\n**Explanation:** This is the **symbol interning** optimization. Instead of using raw strings as keys (which requires character-by-character comparison), every string identifier is converted to a unique `S_symbol` pointer. This means:\n- Extracting an integer hash-key is fast: use the pointer itself as the integer hash-key.\n- Comparing symbols for equality is fast: just compare pointers (or integers).\n- Comparing two symbols for "greater-than" is very fast: pointer/integer comparison (for binary search trees).\n\n### 3.9 Tiger Compiler — Symbol Table Interface\n\n```c\ntypedef struct S_symbol_ *S_symbol;\nS_symbol S_symbol(string);\nstring S_name(S_symbol);\n\ntypedef struct TAB_table_ *S_table;\nS_table S_empty(void);\nvoid S_enter(S_table t, S_symbol sym, void *value);\nvoid *S_look(S_table t, S_symbol sym);\nvoid S_beginScope(S_table t);\nvoid S_endScope(S_table t);\n```\n\n**Explanation:** This defines the public interface for symbols and symbol tables.\n- `S_symbol` — unique identifiers (interned strings).\n- `S_name` — convert a symbol back to its string representation.\n- `S_table` — the symbol table type.\n- `S_empty` — create an empty table.\n- `S_enter` — insert a binding (key → value).\n- `S_look` — look up a key, returning its binding (or NULL).\n- `S_beginScope` / `S_endScope` — manage scope boundaries.\n\n### 3.10 Tiger Compiler — Implementation (Imperative Style)\n\n```c\n// make a new S_Table\nS_table S_empty(void) {\n    return TAB_empty();\n}\n\n// insert a binding\nvoid S_enter(S_table t, S_symbol sym, void *value) {\n    TAB_enter(t, sym, value);\n}\n\n// look up a symbol\nvoid *S_look(S_table t, S_symbol sym) {\n    return TAB_look(t, sym);\n}\n```\n\n**Explanation:** The Tiger compiler uses **destructive-update** (imperative) environments. `TAB_enter` inserts a new binding into the hash table, and `TAB_look` searches for a symbol. These are thin wrappers around the underlying generic hash table module `TAB`.\n\n### 3.11 Tiger Compiler — Scope Management with Marksym\n\n```c\nstatic struct S_symbol_ marksym = { "<mark>", 0 };\n\nvoid S_beginScope(S_table t) {\n    S_enter(t, &marksym, NULL);\n}\n\nvoid S_endScope(S_table t) {\n    S_symbol s;\n    do\n        s = TAB_pop(t);\n    while (s != &marksym);\n}\n```\n\n**Explanation:** To manage scope boundaries in an imperative hash table:\n- `S_beginScope`: inserts a special marker symbol `"<mark>"` into the table.\n- `S_endScope`: pops symbols from the table **backwards** until the marker is found. All bindings added since `beginScope` are removed, effectively restoring the table to its previous state.\n\n### 3.12 Tiger Compiler — Auxiliary Stack\n\n```c\n// Shows in what order the symbols were "pushed" into the symbol table.\n// As each symbol in this stack is popped, the head binding in its bucket is removed.\n\n// beginScope: pushes a special marker onto the stack\n// endScope: pops symbols off the stack until finds the topmost marker\n```\n\n**Explanation:** The auxiliary stack records the order in which symbols were inserted. During `endScope`, the stack is used to determine exactly which bindings to remove. When a symbol is popped from the stack, its corresponding head binding in the collision chain is removed. The marker at the bottom of the popped range delimits how far back to undo.\n\n### 3.13 Tiger Compiler — Integrated Stack in Binder\n\n```c\nstruct TAB_table_ {\n    binder table[TABSIZE];\n    void *top;\n};\n\nt->table[index] = Binder(key, value, t->table[index], t->top);\n\nstatic binder Binder(void *key, void *value, binder next, void *prevtop) {\n    binder b = checked_malloc(sizeof(*b));\n    b->key = key;\n    b->value = value;\n    b->next = next;\n    b->prevtop = prevtop;\n    return b;\n}\n```\n\n**Explanation:** The auxiliary stack is integrated into the `Binder` structure itself, avoiding a separate stack data structure. Each `Binder` node carries a `prevtop` field that points to the most recent symbol bound in the table at the time this binder was created. This allows `endScope` to walk back through the chain using `prevtop` pointers to find and remove the correct bindings.\n\n**Pushing:** Copy `top` into the `prevtop` field of the new Binder.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Multiple Symbol Tables — Functional Symbol Table with Binary Search Trees\n\n### 4.1 Why Hash Tables Don\'t Work Well for Functional Style\n\nWhen computing `m2 = m1 + {mouse ↦ 4}` where `m1 = {bat ↦ 1, camel ↦ 2, dog ↦ 3}`, and `hash(mouse) = hash(camel)`, inserting into the hash table gives:\n```\nhash(mouse) → <mouse, 4>\n```\nBut we have **lost access to m1** because the hash table was modified in place.\n\nCopying the entire array but sharing old buckets is not efficient — hash table arrays should be quite large.\n\n### 4.2 Binary Search Tree Solution\n\n**Principle:** For functional-style additions, use **binary search trees**. When adding a new node at depth `d`, we only need to create `d` new nodes (copying the path from root to parent of the inserted node). The rest of the tree is shared.\n\n- `m1` remains completely unchanged.\n- `m2` shares all nodes with `m1` except those on the insertion path.\n- Lookup cost: `O(log n)` for a balanced tree of `n` nodes.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Homework Assignments — HW.PDF\n\n### Question 1 (Multiple Choice)\n\n> When the compiler exits a scope, it must restore the symbol table. Which implementation accomplishes this where the primary overhead involves manipulating a few pointers?\n\n**Answer: D) Both B and C are correct.**\n\n**Explanation:**\n- **Option B** — Functional-style balanced BST only creates new nodes along the path from root to insertion point (a few pointers), keeping the old tree intact.\n- **Option C** — Imperative hash table with an undo stack reverses modifications by popping entries (a few pointer operations).\n- **Option A** is incorrect because deep copying the entire hash table is expensive.\n\n### Question 2 (Fill in the Blank)\n\n> In the Tiger pseudocode, assume `σ0 = {g ↦ string, a ↦ int}`. What is the type bound to variable `a` when `print(a)` on line 8 is executed?\n\n```n\n```\n\n**Answer: `int`**\n\n**Explanation:**\n- Line 1: `σ1 = σ0 + {a ↦ int, b ↦ int, c ↦ int}` — `a` is bound to `int`.\n- Line 4: `σ3 = σ2 + {a ↦ string}` — `a` is **rebound** to `string` inside the let scope.\n- Line 6: End of let scope — discard `σ3`, go back to `σ1`.\n- Line 8: `print(a)` — `a` is looked up in `σ1`, where `a ↦ int`.\n- Therefore, the type of `a` on line 8 is **`int`**.\n\n### Question 3 (Multiple Choice)\n\n> For an imperative-style hash table with external chaining, what is the most efficient way to handle inserting `a ↦ τ2` when `a ↦ τ1` already exists?\n\n**Answer: C) Insert the new binding at the head of the collision chain.**\n\n**Explanation:** By inserting the new binding at the head, it shadows the old binding (`hash(a) → <a, τ2> → <a, τ1>`). This is exactly what the `insert` function does: `table[index] = Bucket(key, binding, table[index])`. This is efficient (`O(1)`) and compatible with `pop` (which removes the head).\n\n- Option A (overwrite) is less general (would need to find the existing entry first).\n- Option B (append) would break `pop` (which only removes the head).\n- Option D is incorrect — variable redefinition/shadowing is expected and allowed in the language.\n\n### Question 4 (Fill in the Blank)\n\n> The Tiger compiler converts string identifiers into a unique, fixed-size data type to speed up hash calculations and equality checks. What is this data type called?\n\n**Answer: Symbol** (or **symbol**, **S_symbol**, **the Symbol module**, or **symbol pointer**)\n\n**Explanation:** The Tiger compiler\'s Symbol module interns strings into unique `S_symbol` pointer values. After interning, all hash computations use the pointer as the integer hash-key, and equality checks use pointer comparison instead of character-by-character string comparison.\n\n### Question 5 (Multiple Choice)\n\n> When processing Java (with forward references), how does the symbol table system handle classes referencing members defined later?\n\n**Answer: B) Each class has its own symbol table, which are then combined into a single comprehensive environment.**\n\n**Explanation:** As shown in the slides (Slide 8), Java allows forward references because all classes in a package are compiled in a combined environment `σ7 = σ2 + σ4 + σ6`. Each class `E`, `N`, `D` has its own local symbol table (`σ2`, `σ4`, `σ6`), and these are merged into the package-level environment before compiling. This allows `class D` to reference `N.a` even though `D` is defined after `N`.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Summary\n\n| Concept | Key Takeaway |\n|---|---|\n| Functional symbol table | Never mutates; creates new tables; uses BSTs for efficiency |\n| Imperative symbol table | Mutates in place; uses hash tables; undo stack for scope exit |\n| Hash table with chaining | O(1) insert/lookup; insert at head for shadowing; pop head for undo |\n| Symbol interning | Converts strings to unique pointers for fast comparison |\n| Scope management | Marksym or auxiliary stack to track scope boundaries |\n| Multiple environments | Each module/class has its own table; combined for package scope |\n| Forward references | Enabled by combining all class tables into a single environment |\nENDOFFILE\n'] min_timeout_sec: 2.0 max_timeout_sec: 180.0
