Sending keys: ['cat > /app/NOTE.md << \'ENDOFFILE\'\n# Compiler Principles — Lecture Notes (Chapter 5: Semantic Analysis)\n\nThese notes summarize the key points from the presentation **Compile.pptx** and include explanations of the homework assignment **HW.PDF**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Symbol Tables: Functional vs. Imperative Style\n\n### What Is a Symbol Table?\n\nA **symbol table** (also called an *environment*) is a data structure used during semantic analysis that maps **identifiers** (variable/function names) to their **types and locations**.\n\n- **Declarations** bind identifiers to their meanings (e.g., `a ↦ int`).\n- **Uses** look up identifiers in the symbol tables.\n- An environment is a set of bindings denoted by the `↦` arrow.  \n  Example: `σ₀ = {g ↦ string, a ↦ int}`\n\nBindings in the right-hand table **override** those in the left-hand table. Table union `X + Y` is **not** the same as `Y + X`.\n\n### Functional Symbol Table\n\n**Definition:** In the functional style, you **keep the old environment intact (in pristine condition)** and create a **new environment** for each scope. The old environment is preserved as a fallback.\n\n**Insertion operation:**\n- You do **not** modify the existing environment `σ₁`.\n- Instead, you create a new environment `σ₂ = σ₁ + {j ↦ int}`.\n- Because the old environment is preserved, it is **easy to restore** when exiting a scope — you simply discard the new environment and fall back to the old one.\n\n**Under the hood (binary search tree):**\n- If we add a new node at depth `d` of the tree, we **create only `d` new nodes** along the path from the root to the inserted node.\n- We **do not** need to copy the whole tree — just the path from root to the insertion point.\n- Lookup is **O(log n)** for a balanced tree of `n` nodes.\n\n### Imperative Symbol Table\n\n**Definition:** In the imperative style, you **modify the environment in-place** — e.g., modify `σ₁` until it becomes `σ₂`. While `σ₂` exists, you cannot look things up in `σ₁` because `σ₁` has been mutated.\n\n**Insertion operation:**\n- You **mutate** the existing table directly (e.g., insert `<a, int>` into the hash table).\n- There is no separate copy of the old environment.\n\n**Undo mechanism:**\n- To restore the previous state, an **undo stack** is used alongside a single global environment.\n- On entering a scope (`beginScope`), a **special marker** is pushed onto the auxiliary stack.\n- On exiting a scope (`endScope`), symbols are popped from the stack **until the marker** is found. The head binding of each popped symbol\'s bucket is removed, effectively undoing the additions made in that scope.\n\n### Key Differences\n\n| Aspect | Functional Style | Imperative Style |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Mutation** | No mutation; new copies are created | In-place modification of the existing table |\n| **Insertion** | Create new nodes along path from root (tree) | Insert at head of hash chain (hash table) |\n| **Restoring old state** | Simply discard the new environment | Pop items from an undo/auxiliary stack |\n| **Lookup complexity** | O(log n) for balanced tree | O(1) amortized for hash table |\n| **Memory** | May create many nodes per insertion | Only one copy of the table exists |\n| **Efficiency** | Good when few changes per scope | Better for large programs with many identifiers |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Code from the Presentation with Explanations\n\n### 2.1 Efficient Imperative Symbol Tables: Hash Table with External Chaining\n\n```c\nstruct bucket {\n    string key;\n    void *binding;\n    struct bucket *next;\n};\n\n#define SIZE 109\n\nstruct bucket *table[SIZE];\n```\n\n**Explanation:** This defines the core data structure. Each bucket holds a string key, a pointer to the bound value (`void *`), and a pointer to the next bucket in the chain (for resolving hash collisions). The hash table has 109 slots. External chaining means each slot is the head of a linked list of buckets. When two keys hash to the same index, they are linked together — allowing shadowing of older bindings.\n\n---\n\n### 2.2 Hash Function\n\n```c\nunsigned int hash(char *s0)\n{\n    unsigned int h = 0;\n    char *s;\n    for (s = s0; *s; s++)\n        h = h * 65599 + *s;\n    return h;\n}\n```\n\n**Explanation:** This is a multiplicative hash function. For each character `c_i` in the string, it accumulates `h = h * 65599 + c_i`, which is equivalent to the polynomial hash:\n```\nh = α^(n-1)*c_1 + α^(n-2)*c_2 + ... + α*c_{n-1} + c_n\n```\nwhere `α = 65599`. This distributes strings well across hash buckets. The function examines every character of the string, which can be costly — this motivates the Symbol module optimization discussed later.\n\n---\n\n### 2.3 Bucket Constructor\n\n```c\nstruct bucket *Bucket(string key, void *binding, struct bucket *next) {\n    struct bucket *b = checked_malloc(sizeof(*b));\n    b->key = key;\n    b->binding = binding;\n    b->next = next;\n    return b;\n}\n```\n\n**Explanation:** This is a convenience constructor that allocates memory for a new bucket via `checked_malloc` (which likely asserts on failure) and initializes all three fields. It is used by `insert` to prepend new entries to the head of a chain.\n\n---\n\n### 2.4 Insert Operation\n\n```c\nvoid insert(string key, void *binding) {\n    int index = hash(key) % SIZE;\n    table[index] = Bucket(key, binding, table[index]);\n}\n```\n\n**Explanation:** This inserts a binding into the hash table. It hashes the key, takes modulo `SIZE` to get the bucket index, and then creates a new bucket at the **head** of the chain (with `table[index]` as the `next` pointer). This is the **shadowing** behavior: the newest binding for a given key appears first in the chain, so lookups find the most recent one. This naturally handles scope where inner scopes redefine variables.\n\n---\n\n### 2.5 Lookup Operation\n\n```c\nvoid *lookup(string key) {\n    int index = hash(key) % SIZE;\n    struct bucket *b;\n    for (b = table[index]; b; b = b->next)\n        if (0 == strcmp(b->key, key))\n            return b->binding;\n    return NULL;\n}\n```\n\n**Explanation:** This looks up a key by hashing it, finding the correct bucket, and then **traversing the chain** comparing each bucket\'s key using `strcmp`. It returns the binding of the first matching bucket (which is the most recently inserted one due to head-insertion). If no match is found, it returns `NULL`.\n\n---\n\n### 2.6 Pop Operation (for Undo)\n\n```c\nvoid pop(string key) {\n    int index = hash(key) % SIZE;\n    table[index] = table[index]->next;\n}\n```\n\n**Explanation:** This removes the **head** of a bucket chain — essentially undoing the most recent insertion for that key. It works because insertions are done at the head, so popping removes the newest entry, restoring the previous one. However, `pop` alone is not enough for scope management because different variables may have been inserted; we need a smarter mechanism (the auxiliary stack or integrated binder approach).\n\n---\n\n### 2.7 The Symbol Module (Tiger Compiler)\n\n```c\nvoid *:  // We want different notions of binding for different purposes\n         // type bindings for types, value bindings for variables and functions\n\ntypedef struct S_symbol_ *S_symbol;\nS_symbol S_symbol(string);\nstring S_name(S_symbol);\n\ntypedef struct TAB_table_ *S_table;\nS_table S_empty(void);\nvoid S_enter(S_table t, S_symbol sym, void *value);\nvoid *S_look(S_table t, S_symbol sym);\nvoid S_beginScope(S_table t);\nvoid S_endScope(S_table t);\n```\n\n**Explanation:** This is the **interface** for symbols and symbol tables. The key idea is that string identifiers are converted into **unique, fixed-size `S_symbol` pointers**. This means:\n- Extracting a hash key is fast — use the pointer itself as the integer hash key.\n- Comparing symbols for equality is fast — just compare pointers (pointer comparison is O(1)).\n- Comparing symbols for "greater-than" is very fast (for binary search trees).\n\nThe interface provides: create a symbol, convert back to string, create an empty table, enter a binding, look up a binding, and manage scopes.\n\n---\n\n### 2.8 Symbol Creation (String-to-Symbol Conversion)\n\n```c\nstatic S_symbol mksymbol(string name, S_symbol next) {\n    S_symbol s = checked_malloc(sizeof(*s));\n    s->name = name;\n    s->next = next;\n    return s;\n}\n\nS_symbol S_symbol(string name) {\n    int index = hash(name) % SIZE;\n    S_symbol syms = hashtable[index], sym;\n    for (sym = syms; sym; sym = sym->next)\n        if (0 == strcmp(sym->name, name))\n            return sym;\n    sym = mksymbol(name, syms);\n    hashtable[index] = sym;\n    return sym;\n}\n\nstring S_name(S_symbol sym) {\n    return sym->name;\n}\n```\n\n**Explanation:** `mksymbol` is a static helper that allocates and initializes a symbol node. `S_symbol` performs **string interning**: it looks up the string in a secondary hash table. If the string already exists, it returns the existing symbol (pointer equality). Otherwise, it creates a new one. This ensures each unique string has exactly one `S_symbol` instance, making equality checks as simple as pointer comparison.\n\n---\n\n### 2.9 Symbol Table Implementation (Destructive-Update)\n\n```c\n// Make a new S_table\nS_table S_empty(void) {\n    return TAB_empty();\n}\n\n// Insert a binding\nvoid S_enter(S_table t, S_symbol sym, void *value) {\n    TAB_enter(t, sym, value);\n}\n\n// Look up a symbol\nvoid *S_look(S_table t, S_symbol sym) {\n    return TAB_look(t, sym);\n}\n```\n\n**Explanation:** The Tiger compiler uses a **generic hash table module called TAB** under the hood. `S_empty` creates a new empty table, `S_enter` inserts a binding (sym → value), and `S_look` retrieves a binding. The TAB module handles the actual hash table operations. Because `S_symbol` is a pointer, the hash table uses pointer-based hashing and comparison — very efficient.\n\n---\n\n### 2.10 Scope Management: beginScope / endScope\n\n```c\nstatic struct S_symbol_ marksym = { "<mark>", 0 };\n\nvoid S_beginScope(S_table t) {\n    S_enter(t, &marksym, NULL);\n}\n\nvoid S_endScope(S_table t) {\n    S_symbol s;\n    do\n        s = TAB_pop(t);\n    while (s != &marksym);\n}\n```\n\n**Explanation:** This is the **marker-based approach** to scope management.\n- `S_beginScope`: Inserts a special marker symbol `<mark>` into the table.\n- `S_endScope`: Pops bindings one by one using `TAB_pop` until the marker is encountered. All symbols added after the marker (i.e., in that scope) are removed.\n\n**Caveat:** This assumes the marker is always the oldest binding in its bucket when it\'s time to pop — which works because markers are pushed in LIFO order (stack-like behavior) and `TAB_pop` pops the most recent entry globally. However, a **more robust approach** uses an auxiliary stack.\n\n---\n\n### 2.11 Auxiliary Stack Approach\n\n```beginScope:````\n- Pushes a special marker onto a separate auxiliary stack.\n- Tracks which symbols belong to which scope.\n\n```endScope:\n```\n- Pops symbols off the auxiliary stack **until** it finds the topmost marker.\n- As each symbol is popped, the head binding of its bucket in the hash table is removed.\n\n**Explanation:** This is more explicit and robust. The auxiliary stack records every symbol that was entered. When a scope ends, only symbols that were added since the corresponding `beginScope` are removed, regardless of which hash bucket they landed in.\n\n---\n\n### 2.12 Integrated Binder Approach (Pointer-Based Undo)\n\n```c\nstruct TAB_table_ {\n    binder table[TABSIZE];\n    void *top;\n};\n\nt->table[index] = Binder(key, value, t->table[index], t->top);\n\nstatic binder Binder(void *key, void *value, binder next, void *prevtop) {\n    binder b = checked_malloc(sizeof(*b));\n    b->key = key;\n    b->value = value;\n    b->next = next;\n    b->prevtop = prevtop;\n    return b;\n}\n```\n\n**Explanation:** This is the most integrated approach. Instead of a separate undo stack, the table maintains a global `top` pointer tracking the most recently bound entry. Each binder node stores `prevtop` — a pointer to what `top` was **before** this binding was added. This creates a linked list of all insertions. When a scope ends, we can simply walk back through the `prevtop` links to undo bindings, without needing to hash or search. This makes scope exit O(1) per popped binding.\n\n---\n\n### 2.13 Functional Style with Binary Search Trees\n\n**Explanation:** Binary search trees (BSTs) can efficiently support functional additions. When inserting a new node at depth `d`, only `d` new nodes are created (the path from root to the new node\'s parent). The rest of the tree is shared via pointer references with the old version. This means `m1` (the old environment) remains unchanged while `m2` (the new environment) reflects the insertion. Lookup takes **O(log n)** time for a balanced tree of `n` nodes.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Homework Assignment Explanations (HW.PDF)\n\n### Question 1 (Multiple Choice)\n\n**Q:** Which implementation method accomplishes the "restore" operation (when exiting a scope) with primary overhead of manipulating a few pointers, rather than a large-scale copy?\n\n**A) Making a complete, deep copy of the hash-table-based symbol table upon entering the scope.**\n- ❌ Incorrect. This involves a large-scale copy, not a few pointers.\n\n**B) Using a functional-style balanced binary search tree, which only creates new nodes along the path from the root to the insertion point when adding a new binding.**\n- ✅ Correct. Only `d` nodes are created/copied (path-length), and restoring means discarding the new tree root — a single pointer change.\n\n**C) Using an imperative-style hash table combined with a separate "undo stack" to log all modifications, which are then reversed upon exiting the scope.**\n- ✅ Correct. The undo stack pops a few pointers per removal.\n\n**D) Both B and C are correct.**\n- ✅ **Correct Answer: D** — Both approaches achieve efficient restore by manipulating only a few pointers.\n\n---\n\n### Question 2 (Fill in the Blank)\n\n**Q:** In the Tiger pseudocode below, what is the type bound to `a` when `print(a)` on line 8 is executed?\n\n```tiger\n1 function f(a:int, b:int, c:int) =\n2 (print_int(a+c);\n3  let var j := a+b\n4       var a := "hello"\n5  in print(a); print_int(j)\n6  end;\n7  print_int(b)\n8 )\n```\n\n**Answer:** `string`\n\n**Explanation:** The initial environment `σ₀ = {g ↦ string, a ↦ int}`. In the function body (line 1), `a` is initially bound to `int`. Inside the `let` block (line 4), a new local variable `a` is declared and initialized to the string `"hello"`, which **shadows** the outer `a:int`. When `print(a)` executes on line 8 (actually line 5, inside the `let` block), the most recent binding of `a` is `a ↦ string`. So the answer is **string**.\n\n---\n\n### Question 3 (Multiple Choice)\n\n**Q:** What is the most efficient and scope-compliant way to handle inserting a new binding `a ↦ τ₂` when an older binding `a ↦ τ₁` already exists?\n\n**A) Traverse the collision chain to find and overwrite the old binding.**\n- ❌ Incorrect. Overwriting would destroy the outer-scope binding.\n\n**B) Append the new binding to the end of the collision chain.**\n- ❌ Incorrect. Lookup traverses from the head; appending would require traversing the whole chain every time.\n\n**C) Insert the new binding at the head of the collision chain, effectively shadowing the old binding.**\n- ✅ **Correct Answer: C.** Inserting at the head makes the new binding the first one encountered during lookup (shadowing), while the old binding remains in the chain and is visible when the scope exits.\n\n**D) Throw a "variable redefined" compilation error.**\n- ❌ Incorrect. Shadowing is valid in most languages (e.g., local variables can shadow parameters).\n\n---\n\n### Question 4 (Fill in the Blank)\n\n**Q:** The Tiger compiler converts string identifiers into a unique, fixed-size data type to optimize hash calculations and equality checks. This optimized data type is referred to as a ____. \n\n**Answer:** `symbol`\n\n**Explanation:** As described in the presentation, the **Symbol module** converts each string into a unique `S_symbol` pointer. Hashing uses the pointer itself as the integer hash key, equality is pointer comparison, and ordering is also pointer-based. This avoids the cost of character-by-character string comparison.\n\n---\n\n### Question 5 (Multiple Choice)\n\n**Q:** How does the symbol table system handle forward references in Java (e.g., class D referencing class N, which is defined after D)?\n\n```java\npackage M;\nclass E { static int a = 5; }\nclass N { static int b = 10; static int a = E.a + b; }\nclass D { static int d = E.a + N.a; }\n```\n\n**A) A single global symbol table is used.**\n- ❌ Not quite. Each class still gets its own symbol table for its members.\n\n**B) Each class (E, N, D) has its own symbol table, which are then combined (σ₂ + σ₄ + σ₆) into a single comprehensive environment σ₇ representing the entire package.**\n- ✅ **Correct Answer: B.** The presentation explains that all three classes are compiled in environment `σ₇ = σ₂ + σ₄ + σ₆`, and the result of the analysis is `{M ↦ σ₇}`. This combined environment makes all symbols visible to each other, enabling forward references.\n\n**C) Strict sequential order processing.**\n- ❌ Incorrect. This wouldn\'t allow forward references.\n\n**D) A stack-based symbol table.**\n- ❌ Not the approach described in the presentation for this case.\n\n---\n\n*End of notes.*\nENDOFILE\n'] min_timeout_sec: 1.0 max_timeout_sec: 180.0
