Sending keys: ['cat > /app/NOTE.md << \'NOTEEOF\'\n# Compiler Principles – Chapter 5: Semantic Analysis Study Notes\n\n## 1. Symbol Tables: Functional vs. Imperative Style\n\n### 1.1 What is a Symbol Table?\n\nThe **semantic analysis phase** of a compiler connects variable definitions to their uses, checks that each expression has a correct type, and translates the abstract syntax into a simpler representation suitable for generating machine code.\n\nA **symbol table** (also called an **environment**) is a data structure that maps identifiers to their types and locations. It supports three key operations:\n- **Insert** (enter): Bind an identifier to a meaning (type, location, etc.)\n- **Lookup**: Find the binding for a given identifier\n- **Undo/Restore**: Restore the table to a previous state when exiting a scope\n\nAn environment is a set of bindings denoted by the ↦ arrow. For example:\n```\nσ0 = {g ↦ string, a ↦ int}\n```\n\nWhen a new binding is added, we use the notation σ\' = σ + {a ↦ int}, meaning σ\' contains all bindings of σ plus the new binding for `a`.\n\n### 1.2 Functional Symbol Tables\n\n**Definition:** In the **functional style**, symbol tables are kept in **pristine condition**. When a new binding is added, the previous symbol table is preserved unchanged, and a new table is created with the additional binding.\n\n**How insertion works:**\n- When adding a binding `σ2 = σ1 + {j ↦ int}`, a **new copy** of the relevant structure is created.\n- The old table `σ1` remains intact and can be looked up at any time.\n- This is similar to persistent data structures — no mutation occurs.\n\n**Advantages:**\n- Easy to restore previous states (just point back to the earlier table)\n- No need for explicit undo mechanisms\n- Concurrent lookups are safe (no mutation)\n\n**Disadvantages:**\n- Can be expensive in terms of memory and time, as adding a new binding may require copying data\n\n**Implementation:** Functional symbol tables are commonly implemented using **balanced binary search trees**. When a new node is added at depth `d`, only `d` new nodes are created (along the path from root to the insertion point), not the entire tree.\n\n### 1.3 Imperative Symbol Tables\n\n**Definition:** In the **imperative style**, the symbol table is **modified in place**. To add a binding, the existing table is directly updated. To undo the modification (e.g., when exiting a scope), a separate **undo stack** records the changes so they can be reversed.\n\n**How insertion works:**\n- When adding a binding `σ2 = σ1 + {j ↦ int}`, the table `σ1` itself is **mutated** to become `σ2`.\n- While the new binding exists, the old binding for the same key is shadowed.\n- When exiting the scope, the modifications are undone to restore the previous state.\n\n**Advantages:**\n- Very efficient for large programs with thousands of identifiers\n- Lookups are O(1) on average with hash tables\n\n**Disadvantages:**\n- Requires an undo mechanism (stack) to restore previous states\n- While new bindings exist, the old state is lost\n\n**Implementation:** Imperative symbol tables are typically implemented using **hash tables with external chaining**. An **undo stack** (or auxiliary stack) is maintained alongside the hash table to track changes.\n\n### 1.4 Key Differences in Insertion Operations\n\n| Aspect | Functional Style | Imperative Style |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Mutation** | No mutation; old table preserved | Table mutated in place |\n| **Storage** | Creates new nodes for new bindings | Reuses and modifies existing structure |\n| **Restoration** | Simply revert pointer to old table | Use undo stack to reverse changes |\n| **Data Structure** | Balanced binary search tree | Hash table with external chaining |\n| **Insertion cost** | O(log n) — create d new nodes at depth d | O(1) — insert at head of bucket chain |\n| **Lookup cost** | O(log n) for balanced tree | O(1) average with hash table |\n| **Memory** | Higher (copies nodes along path) | Lower (direct insertion) |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Code from the Presentation with Explanations\n\n### 2.1 Tiger Pseudocode Example (Slides 4–6)\n\n```pascal\nfunction f(a:int, b:int, c:int) =\n  (print_int(a+c);\n   let var j := a+b\n        var a := "hello"\n   in print(a); print_int(j)\n   end;\n   print_int(b)\n  )\n```\n\n**Explanation:** This example demonstrates how symbol tables change as we enter and exit scopes.\n\n- **Line 1:** `σ1 = σ0 + {a ↦ int, b ↦ int, c ↦ int}` — parameters `a`, `b`, `c` are bound to `int`\n- **Line 3:** `σ2 = σ1 + {j ↦ int}` — local variable `j` is bound to `int`\n- **Line 4:** `σ3 = σ2 + {a ↦ string}` — inner variable `a` shadows the outer `a` and is bound to `string`\n  - **Key point:** Bindings in the right-hand table **override** those in the left. The `a ↦ int` from `σ1` is now shadowed.\n- **Line 6:** End of `let` scope — discard `σ3`, go back to `σ1` (unshadows `a ↦ int`)\n- **Line 7:** Look up `b` in `σ1` — finds `b ↦ int`\n- **Line 8:** End of function — discard `σ1`, go back to `σ0`\n\n### 2.2 Hash Function and Bucket Structure (Slide 18)\n\n```c\nstruct bucket { string key; void *binding; struct bucket *next; };\n\n#define SIZE 109\n\nstruct bucket *table[SIZE];\n\nunsigned int hash(char *s0)\n{\n    unsigned int h = 0;\n    char *s;\n    for(s = s0; *s; s++)\n        h = h * 65599 + *s;\n    return h;\n}\n\nstruct bucket *Bucket(string key, void *binding, struct bucket *next) {\n    struct bucket *b = checked_malloc(sizeof(*b));\n    b->key = key;\n    b->binding = binding;\n    b->next = next;\n    return b;\n}\n```\n\n**Explanation:**\n- **`struct bucket`**: Each bucket node holds a string `key`, a generic `binding` (pointer to type info, variable info, etc.), and a `next` pointer for **external chaining** (hash collision resolution).\n- **`SIZE = 109`**: The hash table has 109 buckets (109 is a prime number, which helps reduce clustering).\n- **`table[SIZE]`**: An array of pointers to bucket chains. `table[i]` points to the head of the chain for hash bucket `i`.\n- **`hash()` function**: Computes a hash value for a string using polynomial rolling hash: `h = (α^(n-1)*c₁ + α^(n-2)*c₂ + … + α*cₙ₋₁ + cₙ)`, where `α = 65599` and `cᵢ` are the character values. This distributes strings well across buckets.\n- **`Bucket()` constructor**: Allocates memory for a new bucket node and initializes its fields. Takes the key, binding, and the `next` pointer (usually the current head of the chain) as arguments.\n\n### 2.3 Insert, Lookup, and Pop Operations (Slide 19)\n\n```c\nvoid insert(string key, void *binding) {\n    int index = hash(key) % SIZE;\n    table[index] = Bucket(key, binding, table[index]);\n}\n\nvoid *lookup(string key) {\n    int index = hash(key) % SIZE;\n    struct bucket *b;\n    for (b = table[index]; b; b = b->next)\n        if (0 == strcmp(b->key, key))\n            return b->binding;\n    return NULL;\n}\n\nvoid pop(string key) {\n    int index = hash(key) % SIZE;\n    table[index] = table[index]->next;\n}\n```\n\n**Explanation:**\n- **`insert()`**: Computes the hash bucket index, then creates a new bucket node at the **head** of the chain. This is the most efficient approach — O(1) insertion. The new binding shadows any existing binding for the same key.\n- **`lookup()`**: Computes the hash bucket index, then traverses the chain using `strcmp` to find the matching key. Returns the binding if found, or `NULL` if not found.\n- **`pop()`**: Removes the **first** element of the chain (the most recently inserted). This is used for scope exit — the innermost binding is popped off.\n\n> **Note:** The `pop()` function above only removes one element. For proper scope management with arbitrary identifiers, a more sophisticated mechanism (see below) is needed.\n\n### 2.4 Symbol Module Interface (Slide 21)\n\n```c\ntypedef struct S_symbol_ *S_symbol;\nS_symbol S_symbol(string);\nstring S_name(S_symbol);\n\ntypedef struct TAB_table_ *S_table;\nS_table S_empty(void);\nvoid S_enter(S_table t, S_symbol sym, void *value);\nvoid *S_look(S_table t, S_symbol sym);\nvoid S_beginScope(S_table t);\nvoid S_endScope(S_table t);\n```\n\n**Explanation:**\n- **`S_symbol`**: Represents a unique, fixed-size symbol (converted from string identifiers). Using symbols instead of raw strings makes hash computation and equality comparison very fast.\n- **`S_symbol(string)`**: Converts a string to a symbol. If the same string already exists, returns the existing symbol (interning).\n- **`S_name(S_symbol)`**: Converts a symbol back to its string representation (for output/errors).\n- **`S_table`**: The symbol table type.\n- **`S_empty()`**: Creates an empty symbol table.\n- **`S_enter()`**: Inserts a binding (equivalent to `insert`).\n- **`S_look()`**: Looks up a symbol\'s binding (equivalent to `lookup`).\n- **`S_beginScope()`**: Marks the beginning of a new scope (pushes a marker).\n- **`S_endScope()`**: Ends the current scope (pops all bindings back to the marker).\n\n### 2.5 Symbol Implementation (Slide 22)\n\n```c\nstatic S_symbol mksymbol(string name, S_symbol next) {\n    S_symbol s = checked_malloc(sizeof(*s));\n    s->name = name;\n    s->next = next;\n    return s;\n}\n\nS_symbol S_symbol(string name) {\n    int index = hash(name) % SIZE;\n    S_symbol syms = hashtable[index], sym;\n    for (sym = syms; sym; sym = sym->next)\n        if (0 == strcmp(sym->name, name))\n            return sym;\n    sym = mksymbol(name, syms);\n    hashtable[index] = sym;\n    return sym;\n}\n\nstring S_name(S_symbol sym) {\n    return sym->name;\n}\n```\n\n**Explanation:**\n- **`mksymbol()`**: Helper function to allocate and initialize a new symbol node.\n- **`S_symbol(string)`**: The **symbol interning** function. It first checks if a symbol with the given name already exists in the hashtable. If it does, it returns the existing symbol (ensuring each unique string has exactly one symbol). If not, it creates a new symbol node and inserts it at the head of the appropriate bucket chain. This makes string comparisons extremely fast — we just compare symbol pointers instead of doing `strcmp` on every lookup.\n- **`S_name()`**: Retrieves the string name from a symbol, used for error messages and output.\n\n### 2.6 Symbol Table Implementation – Hash Table Module (Slide 23)\n\n```c\nS_table S_empty(void) {\n    return TAB_empty();\n}\n\nvoid S_enter(S_table t, S_symbol sym, void *value) {\n    TAB_enter(t, sym, value);\n}\n\nvoid *S_look(S_table t, S_symbol sym) {\n    return TAB_look(t, sym);\n}\n```\n\n**Explanation:**\n- The Tiger compiler uses **destructive-update environments** (imperative style). The `TAB` module is a generic hash table that wraps the low-level operations.\n- **`TAB_empty()`**: Creates an empty hash table.\n- **`TAB_enter()`**: Inserts a binding into the hash table (new binding at head of chain, shadowing older ones).\n- **`TAB_look()`**: Looks up a symbol in the hash table by traversing the chain.\n\n### 2.7 Scope Management with Undo Stack (Slide 24)\n\n```c\nstatic struct S_symbol_ marksym = { "<mark>", 0 };\n\nvoid S_beginScope(S_table t) {\n    S_enter(t, &marksym, NULL);\n}\n\nvoid S_endScope(S_table t) {\n    S_symbol s;\n    do\n        s = TAB_pop(t);\n    while (s != &marksym);\n}\n```\n\n**Explanation:**\n- **`marksym`**: A special sentinel symbol with the name `"<mark>"`. It serves as a boundary marker in the undo stack.\n- **`S_beginScope()`**: When entering a new scope (e.g., `let ... in ... end`), it inserts the marker symbol into the table. This records the current state.\n- **`S_endScope()`**: When exiting a scope, it repeatedly pops bindings from the table until it encounters the marker. All bindings added after the marker are removed, restoring the previous state.\n- **`TAB_pop()`**: Removes and returns the most recently inserted binding from the hash table.\n\n### 2.8 Auxiliary Stack for Symbol Tracking (Slide 25)\n\n```\nAuxiliary Stack:\nShowing in what order the symbols were "pushed" into the symbol table.\n\nAs each symbol in this stack is popped, the head binding in its bucket is removed.\n\nbeginScope: pushes a special marker onto the stack\nendScope: pops symbols off the stack until it finds the topmost marker.\n```\n\n**Explanation:**\n- The auxiliary stack tracks the **order of insertions** into the symbol table.\n- When `beginScope` is called, a special marker is pushed onto the stack.\n- When `endScope` is called, symbols are popped from the stack one by one. For each popped symbol, the **head binding** in its bucket chain is removed. This continues until the marker is found.\n- This ensures that all bindings added within the current scope are properly removed, regardless of which bucket chain they belong to.\n\n### 2.9 Integrated Top Pointer (Slide 26)\n\n```c\nstatic binder Binder(void *key, void *value, binder next, void *prevtop) {\n    binder b = checked_malloc(sizeof(*b));\n    b->key = key;\n    b->value = value;\n    b->next = next;\n    b->prevtop = prevtop;\n    return b;\n}\n```\n\n```c\nstruct TAB_table_ {\n    binder table[TABSIZE];\n    void *top;\n};\n\nt->table[index] = Binder(key, value, t->table[index], t->top);\n```\n\n**Explanation:**\n- Instead of maintaining a separate auxiliary stack, the **top pointer can be integrated** directly into the binder structure.\n- **`top`**: A global pointer tracking the most recently bound symbol in the table.\n- **`prevtop`**: Each binder stores a pointer to the previous value of `top` at the time of insertion.\n- **Pushing**: When inserting a new binding, the current `top` value is copied into `prevtop` of the new binder. Then `top` is updated to point to the new binder.\n- **Popping**: To undo bindings, follow the `prevtop` chain. The outermost binder\'s `prevtop` points back to the state before any new bindings were added, allowing fast restoration.\n- This is a clever optimization that eliminates the need for a separate undo stack — the binder chain itself encodes the history.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Multiple Symbol Tables (Slides 8–9)\n\n### 3.1 Java Example\n\n```java\npackage M;\nclass E { static int a = 5; }\nclass N { static int b = 10; static int a = E.a + b; }\nclass D { static int d = E.a + N.a; }\n```\n\n**Symbol tables:**\n```\nσ1 = { a ↦ int }\nσ2 = { E ↦ σ1 }\nσ3 = { b ↦ int, a ↦ int }\nσ4 = { N ↦ σ3 }\nσ5 = { d ↦ int }\nσ6 = { D ↦ σ5 }\nσ7 = σ2 + σ4 + σ6\n```\n\n**Explanation:** Each class has its own symbol table (`σ1`, `σ3`, `σ5`). These are then combined into a comprehensive environment `σ7`. In Java, **forward references** are allowed — all classes in a package are compiled together in `σ7`, so references to classes defined later (like `N.a` in class `E`) are valid.\n\n### 3.2 ML Example\n\n```sml\nstructure M = struct\n    structure E = struct\n        val a = 5;\n    end\n    structure N = struct\n        val b = 10\n        val a = E.a + b\n    end\n    structure D = struct\n        val d = E.a + N.a\n    end\nend\n```\n\n**Explanation:** Similar to Java, each structure has its own symbol table. `N` is compiled using `σ0 + σ2`, and `D` is compiled using `σ0 + σ2 + σ4`. The result is `{M ↦ σ7}`.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Homework Assignments (HW.PDF)\n\n### Question 1 (Multiple Choice)\n\n**Question:** During semantic analysis, when the compiler exits a scope, which implementation method accomplishes the "restore" operation where the primary overhead involves manipulating a few pointers?\n\n**Options:**\n- A) Complete deep copy of the hash-table-based symbol table\n- B) Functional-style balanced binary search tree\n- C) Imperative-style hash table with undo stack\n- D) Both B and C\n\n**Answer: D**\n\n**Explanation:**\n- **Option B** (Functional BST): When exiting a scope, you simply revert the pointer back to the previous tree. Only the path nodes were copied, not the entire tree.\n- **Option C** (Imperative + undo stack): You pop elements from the undo stack to reverse changes. This is just pointer manipulation.\n- **Option A** is incorrect because making a complete deep copy is expensive, not pointer-only.\n- Therefore, **both B and C** achieve efficient restoration.\n\n---\n\n### Question 2 (Fill in the Blank)\n\n**Question:** Given σ0 = {g ↦ string, a ↦ int}, and the following code:\n\n```pascal\n1 function f(a:int, b:int, c:int) =\n2   (\n3     print_int(a+c);\n4     let\n5       var j := a+b\n6       var a := "hello"\n7     in\n8       print(a);\n9       print_int(j)\n10    end;\n11    print_int(b)\n12  )\n```\n\nWhat is the type bound to `a` when `print(a)` on **line 8** is executed?\n\n**Answer: string**\n\n**Explanation:** At line 8, we are inside the `let` block (between `in` and `end`). The scope contains:\n- From outer function: `a ↦ int`, `b ↦ int`, `c ↦ int`\n- From `let` body: `j ↦ int`, `a ↦ string` (line 6 shadows the outer `a`)\n\nSince the inner binding `a ↦ string` overrides the outer `a ↦ int`, when `print(a)` executes on line 8, `a` has type **string**.\n\n---\n\n### Question 3 (Multiple Choice)\n\n**Question:** When implementing an imperative-style symbol table using a hash table with external chaining, what is the most efficient and scope-compliant way to handle inserting a new binding `a ↦ τ₂` when an older binding `a ↦ τ₁` already exists?\n\n**Options:**\n- A) Traverse the chain to find and overwrite the old binding\n- B) Append the new binding at the end of the chain\n- C) Insert the new binding at the head of the chain (shadowing)\n- D) Throw a "variable redefined" error\n\n**Answer: C**\n\n**Explanation:**\n- **Option C** is correct. By inserting the new binding `a ↦ τ₂` at the **head** of the collision chain, it shadows the older binding `a ↦ τ₁`. Lookups will find the new binding first (which is the correct semantics — inner scopes shadow outer scopes).\n- This is also the most efficient approach — O(1) insertion at the head.\n- Option A requires traversing the entire chain to find and overwrite, which is slower.\n- Option B puts the new binding at the end, which means lookups must traverse past old bindings first — semantically wrong and inefficient.\n- Option D is incorrect because variable redefinition (shadowing) is a normal and valid language feature, not an error.\n\n---\n\n### Question 4 (Fill in the Blank)\n\n**Question:** To improve efficiency of symbol table operations, the Tiger compiler converts string identifiers into a unique, fixed-size data type. Hash calculations and equality checks are performed on this new type. This optimized data type is referred to in the presentation as a:\n\n**Answer: symbol**\n\n**Explanation:**\n- The Tiger compiler uses the **Symbol** module to convert string identifiers into **symbols** (unique pointer-based identifiers).\n- This provides three key optimizations:\n  1. **Hash key extraction** is very fast — the symbol pointer itself serves as the integer hash key.\n  2. **Equality comparison** is fast — just compare pointers/integers instead of character-by-character string comparison.\n  3. **Ordering comparison** is fast — needed for binary search trees.\n- The presentation interface defines: `S_symbol S_symbol(string)`, `string S_name(S_symbol)`, with symbol interning to ensure each unique string has exactly one symbol.\n\n---\n\n### Question 5 (Multiple Choice)\n\n**Question:** How does a symbol table system handle forward references in Java, where a member of one class can reference a member of a class defined later in source code?\n\n**Options:**\n- A) Single global symbol table with all members\n- B) Each class has its own symbol table, combined into comprehensive environment σ₇\n- C) Strict sequential processing order\n- D) Stack-based symbol table\n\n**Answer: B**\n\n**Explanation:**\n- **Option B** is correct. Each class (E, N, D) has its own symbol table (σ₁, σ₃, σ₅). These are combined into a comprehensive environment `σ₇ = σ₂ + σ₄ + σ₆` that represents the entire package.\n- All classes are compiled within this combined environment `σ₇`, which means all symbols are visible to each other, allowing forward references.\n- Option A is incorrect because Java maintains per-class symbol tables for encapsulation; a single global table would break scoping rules.\n- Option C is incorrect because Java allows forward references — classes don\'t need to be processed in strict source order.\n- Option D is incorrect because the stack-based approach doesn\'t handle cross-class references.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Summary\n\n| Concept | Functional Style | Imperative Style |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Insertion** | Creates new structure, preserves old | Mutates in place |\n| **Restore** | Point to old structure | Undo stack / prevtop chain |\n| **Data Structure** | Balanced BST | Hash table with chaining |\n| **Overhead** | Copy nodes along insertion path | Pointer manipulation |\n| **Tiger Compiler** | Not used | Used (with hash tables) |\n\nThe Tiger compiler implements **imperative-style** symbol tables with:\n1. **Hash tables** for O(1) average-case lookups\n2. **Symbol interning** for fast string-to-symbol conversion\n3. **Auxiliary stack or prevtop integration** for scope management\n4. **External chaining** for hash collision resolution, with new bindings inserted at the head to shadow older ones\nNOTEEOF\n'] min_timeout_sec: 3.0 max_timeout_sec: 180.0
