Sending keys: ['python3 -c "\nwith open(\'/app/NOTE.md\', \'a\') as f:\n    f.write(\'### What is a Symbol Table (Environment)?\\n\')\n    f.write(\'A symbol table (also called an *environment*) maps identifiers to their types and locations. It is a set of **bindings** denoted by the `--` arrow.\\n\\n\')\n    f.write(\'- **Declarations** of identifiers: IDs are bound to meanings in the symbol table.\\n\')\n    f.write(\'- **Uses** of identifiers: look up IDs in the symbol table.\\n\\n\')\n    f.write(\'Example: `sigma_0 = {g --> string, a --> int}`\\n\\n\')\n    f.write(\'### How Symbol Tables Combine\\n\')\n    f.write(\'- `sigma_prd = sigma + {a --> tau}` adds a new binding.\\n\')\n    f.write(\'- **Right-hand bindings override left-hand bindings** when the same identifier appears in both tables.\\n\')\n    f.write(\'- **Order matters**: `X + Y` is not the same as `Y + X`.\\n\\n\')\n    f.write(\'### Scope Management\\n\')\n    f.write(\'Each local variable has a **scope** in which it is visible. As the analysis reaches the end of a scope, the identifier bindings local to that scope are discarded.\\n\\n\')\nprint(\'Part 2 done\')\n"\n'] min_timeout_sec: 1.0 max_timeout_sec: 180.0
Sending keys: ['python3 -c "\nwith open(\'/app/NOTE.md\', \'a\') as f:\n    f.write(\'Example:\\n\')\n    f.write(\'```\\n\')\n    f.write(\'1 function f(a:int, b:int, c:int) =\\n\')\n    f.write(\'2       (print_int(a+c);\\n\')\n    f.write(\'3       let var j := a+b\\n\')\n    f.write(\'4                var a := \\"hello\\"\\n\')\n    f.write(\'5        in  print(a); print_int(j)\\n\')\n    f.write(\'6       end;\\n\')\n    f.write(\'7       print_int(b)\\n\')\n    f.write(\'8       )\\n\')\n    f.write(\'```\\n\')\n    f.write(\'- Line 1: `sigma_1 = sigma_0 + {a --> int, b --> int, c --> int}`\\n\')\n    f.write(\'- Line 3: `sigma_2 = sigma_1 + {j --> int}`\\n\')\n    f.write(\'- Line 4: `sigma_3 = sigma_2 + {a --> string}` (a is re-bound to string, shadowing the int)\\n\')\n    f.write(\'- Line 6: discard `sigma_3`, go back to `sigma_1` (let block ends)\\n\')\n    f.write(\'- Line 8: discard `sigma_1`, go back to `sigma_0` (function ends)\\n\\n\')\nprint(\'Part 3 done\')\n"\n'] min_timeout_sec: 1.0 max_timeout_sec: 180.0
Sending keys: ['python3 -c "\nwith open(\'/app/NOTE.md\', \'a\') as f:\n    f.write(\'---\\n\\n\')\n    f.write(\'## 2. Functional vs Imperative Symbol Tables\\n\\n\')\n    f.write(\'### 2.1 Functional Symbol Table\\n\\n\')\n    f.write(\'**Definition:** A functional symbol table keeps the old table `sigma` in **pristine, unchanged condition** while creating a new, modified table `sigma_prd`. The old table remains fully available for lookups even after the new table is created.\\n\\n\')\n    f.write(\'**Key property:** When computing `sigma_prd = sigma + {a --> tau}`, we **do not modify sigma**. We create a completely new table, producing `sigma_prd` while preserving `sigma` intact. This makes restoring previous states trivial - we just reference the old table.\\n\\n\')\n    f.write(\'**Trade-off:** Creating new data structures for every modification can be costly in memory.\\n\\n\')\n    f.write(\'### 2.2 Imperative Symbol Table\\n\\n\')\n    f.write(\'**Definition:** An imperative symbol table **modifies sigma directly** until it becomes `sigma_prd`. While `sigma_prd` exists, we cannot look things up in the old `sigma` anymore (because sigma has been mutated).\\n\\n\')\n    f.write(\'**Key property:** The table is modified in-place. To restore the previous state, we must **undo** the modifications.\\n\\n\')\n    f.write(\'**Trade-off:** More memory-efficient during execution, but requires additional mechanism (e.g., an undo stack) to restore previous states when exiting scopes.\\n\\n\')\nprint(\'Part 4 done\')\n"\n'] min_timeout_sec: 1.0 max_timeout_sec: 180.0
Sending keys: ['python3 -c "\nwith open(\'/app/NOTE.md\', \'a\') as f:\n    f.write(\'### 2.3 Key Differences in Insertion Operations\\n\\n\')\n    f.write(\'| Aspect | Functional Style | Imperative Style |\\n\')\n    f.write(\'|--------|-----------------|------------------|\\n\')\n    f.write(\'| **Insertion behavior** | Does **not** modify the existing table; creates a **new table** that includes the new binding. | **Modifies** the existing table in-place by adding the new binding. |\\n\')\n    f.write(\'| **Preservation of old state** | The old table remains unchanged and still usable. | The old table is overwritten; it no longer exists as-is. |\\n\')\n    f.write(\'| **Restoring after scope exit** | Simply reference the old table - no undo needed. | Must actively **undo** the modifications (via undo stack or popping bindings). |\\n\')\n    f.write(\'| **Memory overhead** | Higher - new nodes/data structures allocated for each insertion. | Lower - modifies existing structures in-place. |\\n\')\n    f.write(\'| **Implementation approach** | Uses balanced binary search trees; creates only new nodes along the path to the insertion point. | Uses hash tables with external chaining, plus an auxiliary stack for undo. |\\n\\n\')\nprint(\'Part 5 done\')\n"\n'] min_timeout_sec: 1.0 max_timeout_sec: 180.0
