Sending keys: ['cat > /tmp/write_note.py << \'SCRIPTEND\'\nimport textwrap\n\ncontent = textwrap.dedent("""\\\n# Compiler Principles - Lecture Notes\n## Chapter 5: Semantic Analysis (Symbol Tables)\n\n---\n\n## 1. Symbol Table Overview\n\n### What is Semantic Analysis?\nThe semantic analysis phase of a compiler does three things:\n1. **Connects variable definitions to their uses** - resolves which declaration an identifier refers to.\n2. **Checks that each expression has a correct type** - type checking.\n3. **Translates the abstract syntax into a simpler representation** suitable for generating machine code.\n\n### What is a Symbol Table (Environment)?\nA symbol table (also called an *environment*) maps identifiers to their types and locations. It is a set of **bindings** denoted by the `\\u21a0` arrow.\n\n- **Declarations** of identifiers: IDs are bound to meanings in the symbol table.\n- **Uses** of identifiers: look up IDs in the symbol table.\n\nExample: `sigma_0 = {g \\u21a0 string, a \\u21a0 int}`\n\n### How Symbol Tables Combine\n- `sigma\' = sigma + {a \\u21a0 tau}` adds a new binding.\n- **Right-hand bindings override left-hand bindings** when the same identifier appears in both tables.\n- **Order matters**: `X + Y` is not the same as `Y + X`.\n\n### Scope Management\nEach local variable has a **scope** in which it is visible. As the analysis reaches the end of a scope, the identifier bindings local to that scope are discarded.\n\nExample:\n```text\n1 function f(a:int, b:int, c:int) =\n2       (print_int(a+c);\n3       let var j := a+b\n4                var a := "hello"\n5        in  print(a); print_int(j)\n6       end;\n7       print_int(b)\n8       )\n```\n- Line 1: `sigma_1 = sigma_0 + {a \\u21a0 int, b \\u21a0 int, c \\u21a0 int}`\n- Line 3: `sigma_2 = sigma_1 + {j \\u21a0 int}`\n- Line 4: `sigma_3 = sigma_2 + {a \\u21a0 string}` (a is re-bound to string, shadowing the int)\n- Line 6: discard `sigma_3`, go back to `sigma_1` (let block ends)\n- Line 8: discard `sigma_1`, go back to `sigma_0` (function ends)\n\nAt line 7, b is looked up in `sigma_1`.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Functional vs Imperative Symbol Tables\n\n### 2.1 Functional Symbol Table\n\n**Definition:** A functional symbol table keeps the old table `sigma` in **pristine, unchanged condition** while creating a new, modified table `sigma\'`. The old table remains fully available for lookups even after the new table is created.\n\n**Key property:** When computing `sigma\' = sigma + {a \\u21a0 tau}`, we **do not modify sigma**. We create a completely new table, producing `sigma\'` while preserving `sigma` intact. This makes restoring previous states trivial - we just reference the old table.\n\n**Trade-off:** Creating new data structures for every modification can be costly in memory.\n\n### 2.2 Imperative Symbol Table\n\n**Definition:** An imperative symbol table **modifies sigma directly** until it becomes `sigma\'`. While `sigma\'` exists, we cannot look things up in the old `sigma` anymore (because sigma has been mutated).\n\n**Key property:** The table is modified in-place. To restore the previous state, we must **undo** the modifications.\n\n**Trade-off:** More memory-efficient during execution, but requires additional mechanism (e.g., an undo stack) to restore previous states when exiting scopes.\n\n### 2.3 Key Differences in Insertion Operations\n\n| Aspect | Functional Style | Imperative Style |\n|--------|-----------------|------------------|\n| **Insertion behavior** | Does **not** modify the existing table; creates a **new table** that includes the new binding alongside the old one. | **Modifies** the existing table in-place by adding the new binding. |\n| **Preservation of old state** | The old table remains unchanged and still usable. | The old table is overwritten; it no longer exists as-is. |\n| **Restoring after scope exit** | Simply reference the old table - no undo needed. | Must actively **undo** the modifications (via undo stack or popping bindings). |\n| **Memory overhead** | Higher - new nodes/data structures allocated for each insertion. | Lower - modifies existing structures in-place. |\n| **Implementation approach** | Uses balanced binary search trees; creates only new nodes along the path to the insertion point (shares untouched subtrees). | Uses hash tables with external chaining, plus an auxiliary stack for undo. |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Multiple Symbol Tables (Per-Class/Per-Module Environments)\n\nIn some languages (e.g., Java, ML), there can be several **active environments at once**. Each module, class, or record has its own symbol table `sigma` of its own.\n\n### Java Example\n```java\npackage M;\nclass E { static int a = 5; }\nclass N { static int b = 10; static int a = E.a + b; }\nclass D { static int d = E.a + N.a; }\n```\n- `sigma_1 = {a \\u21a0 int}` - class E\'s bindings\n- `sigma_2 = {E \\u21a0 sigma_1}`\n- `sigma_3 = {b \\u21a0 int, a \\u21a0 int}` - class N\'s bindings\n- `sigma_4 = {N \\u21a0 sigma_3}`\n- `sigma_5 = {d \\u21a0 int}` - class D\'s bindings\n- `sigma_6 = {D \\u21a0 sigma_5}`\n- `sigma_7 = sigma_2 + sigma_4 + sigma_6` - combined package environment\n\nIn Java, **forward references are allowed**, so E, N, and D are all compiled in environment `sigma_7`. The result is `{M \\u21a0 sigma_7}`.\n\n### ML Example (SML)\n```sml\nstructure M = struct\n  structure E = struct\n    val a = 5;\n  end\n  structure N = struct\n    val b = 10\n    val a = E.a + b\n  end\n  structure D = struct\n    val d = E.a + N.a\n  end\nend\n```\n- N is compiled using `sigma_0 + sigma_2`.\n- D is compiled using `sigma_0 + sigma_2 + sigma_4`.\n- The result is also `{M \\u21a0 sigma_7}`.\n\nEach class/structure has its own symbol table.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Imperative Symbol Table Implementation (Hash Table with External Chaining)\n\n### 4.1 Core Data Structures\n\n```c\nstruct bucket {\n    string key;\n    void *binding;\n    struct bucket *next;\n};\n#define SIZE 109\nstruct bucket *table[SIZE];\n```\n\n**Explanation:** Each hash table bucket is a **linked list** ("external chaining"). When two keys hash to the same index, they form a chain of buckets. This allows multiple bindings for the same key (for shadowing within scopes). SIZE is 109 (a prime number for good hash distribution).\n\n### 4.2 Hash Function\n\n```c\nunsigned int hash(char *s0) {\n    unsigned int h = 0;\n    char *s;\n    for (s = s0; *s; s++)\n        h = h * 65599 + *s;\n    return h;\n}\n```\n\n**Explanation:** This is a **polynomial rolling hash**. Each character c_i of the string contributes to the hash value with increasing weight of the constant alpha = 65599. Formula: h = (alpha^{n-1} * c_1 + alpha^{n-2} * c_2 + ... + alpha^1 * c_{n-1} + c_n). This provides good distribution of hash values, minimizing collisions.\n\n### 4.3 Bucket Constructor\n\n```c\nstruct bucket *Bucket(string key, void *binding, struct bucket *next) {\n    struct bucket *b = checked_malloc(sizeof(*b));\n    b->key = key;\n    b->binding = binding;\n    b->next = next;\n    return b;\n}\n```\n\n**Explanation:** Allocates a new bucket node using malloc and links it at the front of an existing chain. Returns the new head of the chain.\n\n### 4.4 Insert Operation\n\n```c\nvoid insert(string key, void *binding) {\n    int index = hash(key) % SIZE;\n    table[index] = Bucket(key, binding, table[index]);\n}\n```\n\n**Explanation:** Compute the hash index, then insert the new binding at the **head** of the collision chain at that index. This is crucial: it means the **newest binding for a key shadows all older bindings** for that same key. This implements scope shadowing naturally - a later declaration of the same variable name hides earlier ones.\n\n### 4.5 Lookup Operation\n\n```c\nvoid *lookup(string key) {\n    int index = hash(key) % SIZE;\n    struct bucket *b;\n    for (b = table[index]; b; b = b->next)\n        if (0 == strcmp(b->key, key))\n            return b->binding;\n    return NULL;\n}\n```\n\n**Explanation:** Compute the hash, then **traverse the chain** from head to tail, comparing each key with strcmp. The first match wins - which is always the newest binding due to insert-at-head. Returns NULL if not found.\n\n### 4.6 Pop Operation (for scope exit)\n\n```c\nvoid pop(string key) {\n    int index = hash(key) % SIZE;\n    table[index] = table[index].next;\n}\n```\n\n**Explanation:** Removes the **head** of the bucket chain - the most recently inserted binding for that key. This is how imperative symbol tables achieve **undo** functionality: simply popping the head of the chain restores the previous state (the next binding in the chain becomes the visible one).\n\n### 4.7 Shadowing Behavior\n\nWhen `sigma + {a \\u21a0 tau2}` is performed and sigma already contains `a \\u21a0 tau1`:\n```\nhash(a) -> <a, tau2> -> <a, tau1>\n```\n- Lookup finds tau2 first (correct shadowing behavior).\n- When `pop(a)` is called at the end of scope, sigma is restored:\n```\nhash(a) -> <a, tau1>\n```\n- Insert and pop work in a **stack-like fashion**.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Functional Symbol Table Implementation (Balanced Binary Search Trees)\n\n### 5.1 Why Not Hash Tables for Functional Style?\n\nHash tables are difficult to use functionally:\n```\nm1 = {bat \\u21a0 1, camel \\u21a0 2, dog \\u21a0 3}\nm2 = m1 + {mouse \\u21a0 4}\n```\nIf hash(camel) = hash(mouse) = 5 (hash collision), adding {mouse \\u21a0 4} would modify the existing bucket, destroying m1.\n\n- Copying the entire hash table array to preserve m1 is **too expensive** (the array must be quite large).\n- We cannot afford full copies for every insertion.\n\n### 5.2 Binary Search Tree Solution\n\n**Key idea:** We can perform functional additions to search trees efficiently using **binary search trees (BSTs)**. When we add a new node at depth d, we create only d new nodes - one on the path from root to the insertion point. **Untouched subtrees are shared** between the old and new trees.\n\n**Example structure:**\n```\nm1 (before):           m2 (after adding mouse \\u21a0 4):\n     dog|3                   dog|3\n    /     \\                 /     \\\n  bat|1   camel|2         bat|1   camel|2\n            \\                                   \\\n           mouse|4                          mouse|4\n                                                  |\n                                                 m1 (shared)\n```\n\n- **m1 remains completely unchanged** - it still has its original structure.\n- **m2** is a new tree that shares all unchanged subtrees with m1.\n- Looking up an element: **O(log n)** for a balanced tree of n nodes.\n- Only **d new nodes** are created (where d = depth of insertion).\n\n---\n\n## 6. Symbols in the Tiger Compiler\n\n### 6.1 The Symbol Optimization Problem\n\nIn Program 5.2 of the Tiger compiler source:\n- Every call to the hash function must **examine every character of the string** s.\n- Each comparison with a string in the i-th bucket also examines characters.\n- This is wasteful for frequently-used identifiers.\n\n### 6.2 The Solution: Convert Strings to Symbols\n\nConvert each string identifier to a **symbol** - a unique, fixed-size data type.\n\n**Symbol module properties:**\n1. **Extracting an integer hash-key is very fast**: Use the symbol pointer itself as the integer hash-key (for hash tables).\n2. **Comparing symbols for equality is fast**: Just pointer or integer comparison - no character-by-character strcmp.\n3. **Comparing two symbols for greater-than** (in some arbitrary ordering) is very fast (for binary search trees).\n\n### 6.3 Symbol Interface\n\n```c\n/* Different notions of binding for different purposes in the compiler\n   - type bindings for types, value bindings for variables/functions */\n\ntypedef struct S_symbol_ *S_symbol;\nS_symbol S_symbol(string);\nstring S_name(S_symbol);\n\ntypedef struct TAB_table_ *S_table;\nS_table S_empty(void);\nvoid S_enter(S_table t, S_symbol sym, void *value);\nvoid *S_look(S_table t, S_symbol sym);\nvoid S_beginScope(S_table t);\nvoid S_endScope(S_table t);\n```\n\n**Explanation:** This is the **public interface** for the symbol and symbol table modules.\n- `S_symbol(string)` converts a C string into a unique symbol (canonicalized).\n- `S_name(S_symbol)` converts a symbol back to its string representation.\n- `S_empty()` creates a new, empty symbol table.\n- `S_enter(t, sym, value)` inserts a binding into the table.\n- `S_look(t, sym)` looks up a symbol in the table.\n- `S_beginScope(t)` marks the beginning of a new scope.\n- `S_endScope(t)` removes all bindings added since the most recent S_beginScope.\n\n### 6.4 Symbol Implementation (Canonicalization)\n\n```c\nstatic S_symbol mksymbol(string name, S_symbol next) {\n    S_symbol s = checked_malloc(sizeof(*s));\n    s->name = name;\n    s->next = next;\n    return s;\n}\n\nS_symbol S_symbol(string name) {\n    int index = hash(name) % SIZE;\n    S_symbol syms = hashtable[index], sym;\n    for (sym = syms; sym; sym = sym->next)\n        if (0 == strcmp(sym->name, name))\n            return sym;\n    sym = mksymbol(name, syms);\n    hashtable[index] = sym;\n    return sym;\n}\n\nstring S_name(S_symbol sym) {\n    return sym->name;\n}\n```\n\n**Explanation:** The symbol implementation uses **canonicalization** (string interning). When `S_symbol(string)` is called:\n1. Compute the hash of the string and find the bucket.\n2. **Scan the bucket chain** to see if a symbol with this string already exists (using strcmp).\n3. If found, **return the existing symbol** - this ensures each unique string maps to exactly one symbol object.\n4. If not found, create a new symbol and insert it into the bucket chain.\n\nThis means that "a" is always the **same symbol object** regardless of where or how many times S_symbol("a") is called. This enables fast comparison (pointer equality) instead of string comparison.\n\n### 6.5 Imperative Symbol Table Implementation in the Tiger Compiler\n\nThe Tiger compiler in C uses **destructive-update environments**. An imperative table is implemented using a hash table.\n\n```c\n/* TAB: a generic hash table module */\n\n// Make a new symbol table\nS_table S_empty(void) {\n    return TAB_empty();\n}\n\n// Insert a binding\nvoid S_enter(S_table t, S_symbol sym, void *value) {\n    TAB_enter(t, sym, value);\n}\n\n// Look up a symbol\nvoid *S_look(S_table t, S_symbol sym) {\n    return TAB_look(t, sym);\n}\n```\n\n**Explanation:** These are **wrapper functions** that delegate to the generic TAB (table) module. The core insertion and lookup are delegated to TAB_enter and TAB_look.\n\n### 6.6 Scope Management: beginScope and endScope\n\n```c\nstatic struct S_symbol_ marksym = {"<mark>", 0};\n\nvoid S_beginScope(S_table t) {\n    S_enter(t, &marksym, NULL);\n}\n\nvoid S_endScope(S_table t) {\n    S_symbol s;\n    do\n        s = TAB_pop(t);\n    while (s != &marksym);\n}\n```\n\n**Explanation:** This implements **scope delimitation** using a **marker-based** approach.\n\n- **S_beginScope(t):** Inserts a special marker symbol "<mark>" into the hash table with a NULL value. This marker serves as a sentinel to know where the current scope began.\n\n- **S_endScope(t):** Pops entries off the hash table one by one (using TAB_pop) until it finds the marker. All bindings added after the marker are removed.\n\n### 6.7 Auxiliary Stack for Exact Pop\n\n**Auxiliary stack approach:** Show in what order the symbols were "pushed" into the symbol table. As each symbol in this stack is popped, the head binding in its bucket is removed.\n\n- `beginScope`: pushes a special marker onto the stack.\n- `endScope`: pops symbols off the stack until it finds the topmost marker.\n\n### 6.8 Integrated Approach (Global Top Pointer)\n\nThe auxiliary stack can be integrated into the **Binder** by having a global variable `top` showing the most recent Symbol bound in the table.\n\n```c\nstruct TAB_table_ {\n    binder table[TABSIZE];\n    void *top;\n};\n\nt->table[index] = Binder(key, value, t->table[index], t->top);\n```\n\n```c\nstatic binder Binder(void *key, void *value, binder next, void *prevtop) {\n    binder b = checked_malloc(sizeof(*b));\n    b->key = key;\n    b->value = value;\n    b->next = next;\n    b->prevtop = prevtop;\n    return b;\n}\n```\n\n**Explanation:** This is an **integrated approach** that avoids a separate auxiliary stack.\n\n- Each binder node stores not only key, value, and next (for the chain), but also **prevtop** - a pointer to the previous top value.\n- **Pushing** a new binding: set prevtop to the current top, then update top to point to the new binder.\n- **Popping** all entries in a scope: follow the prevtop links to find the previous scope\'s top value, then pop all binders between the current top and the saved prevtop.\n\nThis elegantly tracks the scope depth using just the existing data structure, without needing a separate stack.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Homework Assignment (HW.PDF) Explanation\n\nThe homework contains 5 questions based on Chapter 5.\n\n### Question 1 (Multiple Choice) - Restoring Symbol Table State\n\n**Question:** When the compiler exits a scope, it must restore the symbol table to its state before entering that scope. Which implementation accomplishes this "restore" operation where the primary overhead involves manipulating a few pointers?\n\n- **A)** Deep copy of hash table on every scope entry.\n- **B)** Functional-style balanced BST (creates new nodes only along the path from root to insertion point).\n- **C)** Imperative-style hash table + undo stack to log and reverse modifications.\n- **D)** Both B and C are correct.\n\n**Answer: D**\n\n**Explanation:** Both B and C are valid approaches. **Approach B (functional BST)** naturally supports restoration because the old tree is preserved - exiting a scope just means discarding the new tree and reusing the old one. Only O(d) nodes are created on the path (d = depth). **Approach C (imperative + undo stack)** works by logging modifications and reversing them. Both only manipulate pointers (not full data structure copies), satisfying the requirement.\n\n---\n\n### Question 2 (Fill in the Blank) - Tiger Shadowing\n\n**Question:** In the Tiger pseudocode below, assume initial sigma_0 = {g \\u21a0 string, a \\u21a0 int}. What is the type bound to the variable a when print(a) on line 8 is executed?\n\n```text\n1  function f(a:int, b:int, c:int) =\n2      (\n3          print_int(a+c);\n4          let\n5              var j := a+b\n6              var a := "hello"\n7          in\n8              print(a);\n9              print_int(j)\n10         end;\n11         print_int(b)\n12     )\n```\n\n**Answer: string**\n\n**Explanation:** Let\'s trace the environments:\n- At function entry: `sigma_1 = sigma_0 + {a \\u21a0 int, b \\u21a0 int, c \\u21a0 int}`. Here a is an int.\n- Inside the let block: `var a := "hello"` creates `sigma_3 = sigma_2 + {a \\u21a0 string}`, shadowing the outer a.\n- `print(a)` on line 8 is **inside** the let block, so it looks up a in `sigma_3`, which binds a to **string**.\n\n---\n\n### Question 3 (Multiple Choice) - Imperative Hash Table Insertion\n\n**Question:** When implementing an imperative-style symbol table using a hash table with external chaining, what is the most efficient and scope-compliant way to handle inserting a \\u21a0 tau2 when a \\u21a0 tau1 already exists?\n\n- **A)** Traverse and overwrite the old binding.\n- **B)** Append to the end of the chain.\n- **C)** Insert at the **head** of the chain (shadowing the old binding).\n- **D)** Throw a "variable redefined" error.\n\n**Answer: C**\n\n**Explanation:** The lecture explicitly states that insertion at the **head** of the collision chain is the correct approach. This:\n1. **Shadows** the old binding naturally (head is checked first during lookup).\n2. Is **O(1)** - just allocate a new bucket and link it.\n3. Works perfectly with **pop** - popping the head restores the previous binding.\n4. Implements **stack-like behavior** that is scope-compliant (LIFO: last in, first out).\n\nOption A is wrong because overwriting loses the old binding and makes undo impossible. Option B is wasteful (O(n) to append). Option D is wrong because variable shadowing is a valid language feature.\n\n---\n\n### Question 4 (Fill in the Blank) - Tiger Symbol Optimization\n\n**Question:** The Tiger compiler converts string identifiers into a unique, fixed-size data type. All subsequent hash calculations and equality checks are performed on this new type. What is this optimized data type called?\n\n**Answer: symbol**\n\n**Explanation:** The presentation\'s Section 6 discusses this optimization. The Tiger compiler introduces the concept of a **symbol** - each string identifier is converted into a unique, fixed-size symbol object. This enables:\n- Fast hash-key extraction (use the symbol pointer as the key).\n- Fast equality comparison (pointer comparison instead of strcmp).\n- Fast ordering comparison (for binary search trees).\n\n---\n\n### Question 5 (Multiple Choice) - Forward References in Java\n\n**Question:** When processing Java (forward references allowed), how is the symbol table system set up so all symbols are visible?\n\n```java\npackage M;\nclass E { static int a = 5; }\nclass N { static int b = 10; static int a = E.a + b; }\nclass D { static int d = E.a + N.a; }\n```\n\n- **A)** A single global symbol table, all members placed directly into it.\n- **B)** Each class has its own symbol table, then combined (sigma_2 + sigma_4 + sigma_6) into a comprehensive environment sigma_7.\n- **C)** Strict sequential order processing.\n- **D)** Stack-based symbol table.\n\n**Answer: B**\n\n**Explanation:** The lecture\'s Slide 8 shows this exact approach:\n1. Class E is compiled with its own `sigma_1 = {a \\u21a0 int}`.\n2. Each class has its **own** symbol table containing only its own members.\n3. These tables are then **combined** into a single comprehensive environment `sigma_7 = sigma_2 + sigma_4 + sigma_6` that represents the entire package.\n4. All classes are compiled using this combined environment `sigma_7`, which allows forward references (e.g., class N can reference E.a even though E and N are defined side-by-side).\n""")\n\nwith open(\'/app/NOTE.md\', \'w\') as f:\n    f.write(content)\n\nprint(f\'NOTE.md written successfully: {len(content)} chars\')\nSCRIPTEND\n'] min_timeout_sec: 1.0 max_timeout_sec: 180.0
